  <HOMEWORK title="Homework 16: Chasing a tone that isn't there" prompt="BER on this link is worse than expected, and a colleague is already convinced it's a new EMI source coupling in from somewhere on the board - they want to start probing around with a near-field H-field probe to find it. Before spending a day chasing hardware, check whether there's actually a dominant periodic component to chase in the first place."
            hint="Look at the Jitter / Phase Noise Scope's spectrum - is there a clear, narrow spike standing out above the noise floor, or does the whole plot look like a fairly flat, noisy floor? Check the TIE histogram's shape too."
            explanation="There's no dominant tone here - the spectrum is broadly flat (noisy, but without a standout spike), and the TIE histogram has a smooth, bell-like (roughly Gaussian) shape rather than the sharper, more bounded shape a strong sinusoidal component would produce. This link's jitter is dominated by elevated broadband random jitter (RJ) - e.g. a noisier-than-expected reference clock synthesizer or wideband supply noise - not a specific coupled aggressor. This is a genuinely useful negative result: confirming the absence of a periodic/EMI cause before spending real lab time physically hunting for a phantom tone that was never there. The place to look instead is broadband noise sources - a clock synthesizer's phase-noise floor, supply ripple's noise floor, thermal effects - not a single coupled clock. The lesson isn't &quot;it's never EMI&quot; (homework 14 shows a case where it genuinely was) - it's that you look at the spectrum either way instead of assuming."/>
</LABCHAIN>
